<div class="container"><div class="lesson-body">
<h2>Mathematical Finance — Black-Scholes, Stochastic Calculus & Portfolio Optimization</h2>
<p>Mathematical finance applies stochastic calculus and optimization theory to model financial markets. The <strong>Black-Scholes-Merton</strong> framework derives the option pricing PDE from a no-arbitrage hedging argument, leading to closed-form prices for European options. <strong>Stochastic calculus</strong> — particularly <strong>Itô's lemma</strong> and the <strong>geometric Brownian motion</strong> model — provides the language for asset price dynamics. The <strong>Fundamental Theorem of Asset Pricing</strong> links the absence of arbitrage to the existence of an equivalent martingale measure (risk-neutral measure). <strong>Portfolio theory</strong> (Markowitz, CAPM, Merton) addresses optimal resource allocation under uncertainty. <strong>Interest rate models</strong> (Vasicek, CIR, HJM) and <strong>credit risk models</strong> (structural and reduced-form) extend the framework to fixed income and defaultable securities.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Financial economics is the study of how people allocate resources over time."</div><div class="attribution">— Robert C. Merton</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>📏 Stochastic Calculus and Geometric Brownian Motion</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Stochastic Calculus</div><div class="story-title">Wiener Process, Itô's Lemma, and Geometric Brownian Motion</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>Wiener process</strong> (Brownian motion) W_t is a continuous stochastic process with independent increments: W_{t+s} − W_t ∼ N(0, s). Its covariance is E[W_t W_s] = min(t, s). The quadratic variation [W]_t = t. For an Itô process dX = μ dt + σ dW, <strong>Itô's lemma</strong> states that for any twice-differentiable f(X, t), df = (∂f/∂t + μ ∂f/∂X + ½σ² ∂²f/∂X²) dt + σ (∂f/∂X) dW. The key second-order term ½σ²∂²f/∂X² arises because (dW)² → dt. A <strong>geometric Brownian motion</strong> (GBM) satisfies dS = μS dt + σS dW. By Itô's lemma applied to f = ln S, the explicit solution is S_t = S_0 exp((μ − σ²/2)t + σW_t). Hence S_t is <strong>log-normally distributed</strong>: E[S_t] = S_0 e^{μt} and Var(S_t) = S_0² e^{2μt}(e^{σ²t} − 1). Under the <strong>risk-neutral measure</strong> Q, the discounted asset price e^{−rt}S_t is a martingale, meaning dS = rS dt + σS dW^Q.</div><div class="story-moral">Itô's lemma is the chain rule of stochastic calculus — the quadratic variation of Brownian motion introduces a second-order correction absent in ordinary calculus.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🌍 Option Pricing and the Black-Scholes Formula</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Option Pricing</div><div class="story-title">Black-Scholes PDE, Feynman-Kac, and the Greeks</div><div class="story-text">Consider a European call option V(S, t) on an asset following GBM. Construct a <strong>hedged portfolio</strong> Π = V − ΔS, choosing Δ = ∂V/∂S to eliminate stochastic risk. By Itô's lemma and the no-arbitrage condition dΠ = rΠ dt, we obtain the <strong>Black-Scholes PDE</strong>: ∂V/∂t + ½σ²S²∂²V/∂S² + rS∂V/∂S − rV = 0. The <strong>Feynman-Kac representation</strong> gives V(S, t) = e^{−r(T−t)} E^Q[Φ(S_T) | S_t = S] where Φ is the payoff. The <strong>Black-Scholes formula</strong> for a European call is C = S·N(d₁) − K·e^{−r(T−t)}·N(d₂) where d₁ = (ln(S/K) + (r + σ²/2)(T−t)) / (σ√(T−t)) and d₂ = d₁ − σ√(T−t). The <strong>Greeks</strong> quantify sensitivity: Δ = N(d₁) (delta), Γ = N'(d₁)/(Sσ√(T−t)) (gamma), Θ = −(SσN'(d₁))/(2√(T−t)) − rKe^{−r(T−t)}N(d₂) (theta), ν = S√(T−t)N'(d₁) (vega). <strong>Put-call parity</strong>: C − P = S − Ke^{−r(T−t)}. The <strong>Cox-Ross-Rubinstein binomial tree</strong> discretizes time, allowing the asset to move up or down each period; the option value is computed by backward induction under risk-neutral probabilities.</div><div class="story-moral">The Black-Scholes PDE is derived from dynamic hedging — eliminating risk by holding the right number of shares, then applying the no-arbitrage principle.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🧮 Portfolio Theory and CAPM</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Portfolio Theory</div><div class="story-title">Markowitz Optimization, Efficient Frontier, and the CAPM</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Markowitz mean-variance optimization</strong> finds the portfolio weights w that minimize risk for a given return: min_w ½ w^T Σ w subject to w^T μ = μ₀ and w^T 1 = 1. The Lagrangian yields w = (Σ^{−1}(μ − λ_f 1))/γ where γ is the risk aversion coefficient. The set of optimal portfolios traces the <strong>efficient frontier</strong> in risk-return space. The <strong>Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)</strong> asserts that in equilibrium, the expected excess return of any asset is proportional to its systematic risk: E[R_i] − R_f = β_i (E[R_M] − R_f) where β_i = Cov(R_i, R_M)/Var(R_M). The <strong>Security Market Line</strong> plots expected return against beta. Extensions include <strong>Merton's intertemporal CAPM (ICAPM)</strong> — which adds state variables that predict future investment opportunities — and the <strong>consumption-based CAPM (CCAPM)</strong> linking asset returns to consumption growth. The <strong>Black-Litterman model</strong> combines CAPM equilibrium returns with investor views using a Bayesian framework.</div><div class="story-moral">The CAPM separates risk into systematic (market) risk, which is priced, and idiosyncratic risk, which is diversified away — beta is the sole measure of risk.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🔥 Interest Rate Models and Credit Risk</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Fixed Income & Credit</div><div class="story-title">Term Structure Models, HJM Framework, and Credit Risk</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>term structure of interest rates</strong> is the relationship between yield and maturity, giving zero-coupon bond prices P(t, T). <strong>Short rate models</strong> describe the instantaneous rate r_t. The <strong>Vasicek model</strong>: dr = a(b − r)dt + σ dW — mean-reverting Gaussian with explicit bond prices. The <strong>Cox-Ingersoll-Ross (CIR) model</strong>: dr = a(b − r)dt + σ√r dW — the square-root diffusion keeps r positive; r_t follows a non-central chi-squared distribution and bond prices have closed-form affine solutions. The <strong>Heath-Jarrow-Morton (HJM) framework</strong> models the entire forward rate curve f(t, T); the no-arbitrage condition relates the drift to the volatility: drift = volatility × ∫_t^T volatility du. The <strong>LIBOR market model (Brace-Gatarek-Musiela)</strong> models forward LIBOR rates. For <strong>credit risk</strong>, <strong>Merton's structural model</strong> treats firm value V_t as a GBM; default occurs at debt maturity T if V_T < D (the debt face value). The equity is a call option on firm assets. The <strong>Black-Cox model</strong> extends this to first-passage default when V_t crosses a barrier. The <strong>reduced-form approach</strong> models default as a surprise event with intensity λ_t; survival probability P[τ > t] = E[exp(−∫_0^t λ_s ds)].</div><div class="story-moral">Interest rate models and credit risk models are essential for pricing fixed-income securities and defaultable claims — they form the backbone of modern fixed-income markets.</div></div>
