 = d₁ − σ√(T−t). The formula is C = S·N(d₁) − Ke^{−r(T−t)}·N(d₂)."},{id:43582,text:"🌍 The delta Δ of a European call option (Black-Scholes) is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"N(d₁)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"N(d₂)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"N'(d₁)/(Sσ√(T−t))",c:false},{l:"D",t:"e^{−r(T−t)}N(d₂)",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Delta</strong> Δ = ∂C/∂S = N(d₁). Gamma Γ = N'(d₁)/(Sσ√(T−t)). Vega ν = S√(T−t)N'(d₁)."},{id:43583,text:"🌍 Put-call parity for European options states:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"C − P = S − Ke^{−r(T−t)}",c:true},{l:"B",t:"C + P = S + Ke^{−r(T−t)}",c:false},{l:"C",t:"C − P = S + Ke^{−r(T−t)}",c:false},{l:"D",t:"C + P = S − Ke^{−r(T−t)}",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Put-call parity</strong>: C − P = S − Ke^{−r(T−t)}. This follows from a portfolio argument: a call plus cash equals a put plus the stock."},{id:43584,text:"🧮 Markowitz mean-variance optimization minimizes:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"½ w^T Σ w subject to return and budget constraints",c:true},{l:"B",t:"w^T μ subject to a risk constraint",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Σ w subject to w^T 1 = 1",c:false},{l:"D",t:"The Sharpe ratio directly",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Markowitz</strong> minimizes ½ w^T Σ w (portfolio variance) subject to w^T μ = μ₀ (target return) and w^T 1 = 1 (budget constraint)."},{id:43585,text:"🧮 In CAPM, beta β_i is defined as:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Cov(R_i, R_M)/Var(R_M)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Var(R_i)/Var(R_M)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Cov(R_i, R_M)/Var(R_i)",c:false},{l:"D",t:"E[R_i]/E[R_M]",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Beta</strong> β_i = Cov(R_i, R_M)/Var(R_M) measures the sensitivity of asset i to market portfolio returns. CAPM: E[R_i]−R_f = β_i(E[R_M]−R_f)."},{id:43586,text:"🧮 The Security Market Line plots:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Expected return against beta",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Expected return against standard deviation",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Variance against expected return",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Beta against variance",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Security Market Line</strong> (SML) graphs E[R_i] versus β_i. The efficient frontier (not the SML) plots return vs standard